<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jingdezhen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Changnan, Fuliang, Porcelain Capital</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jingdezhen is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">Porcelain</a> Capital for producing ceramics for at least 1,000 years. Founded as Changnan under the Jin dynasty, it took its present name in 1004 from the era name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhenzong-of-song">Emperor Zhenzong of Song</a>, under whom its porcelain first rose to fame. Close to deposits of pottery stone and kaolin and to pine forests for fuel, it was the largest centre of Chinese porcelain by the 14th century, making qingbai ware, blue and white from the 1330s and famille rose under the Qing. From the Ming onwards its official kilns made imperial porcelain for the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> forces destroyed its 9,000 kilns in 1855.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jingdezhen lies in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> province, on the Poyang Lake Plain, bordering Anhui. It looks an unpromising place for potteries, a remote town in hilly country, but it stands close to the best deposits of petuntse, or pottery stone, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and is surrounded by forests, mostly of pine, that fed the kilns. The Chang River carried fragile wares down to Poyang Lake and on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, from where they could reach the coast for export.</p><p>Kaolin, which the local kilns first used together with petuntse to make hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, takes its name from the French Jesuit d'Entrecolles's rendering of the local term gaolingtu, from Gaoling, a village in Fuliang County that supplied most of the kaolin in the early and mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area belonged to the state of Chu, then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Han empires. The town was renamed Fuliang in 742 CE and Jingdezhen in 1004. In the Ming and Qing dynasties it counted as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s four great towns for commerce and industry, with Foshan, Hankou and Zhuxian. From the Ming period official kilns there were controlled by the emperor and made imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> in large quantity for the court and for imperial gifts; the imperial kilns stood in the centre of the town at Zhushan (Pearl Hill), with many other kilns four kilometres away at Hutian.</p><p>Its best-known wares were successively Qingbai ware in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan dynasties, blue and white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> from the 1330s, and famille rose and other famille colours under the Qing. It made a great variety of pottery for the Chinese market and for export. In 1855, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, Taiping forces destroyed all 9,000 kilns, which were rebuilt after the war in 1866. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> the town produced many porcelain Mao badges and statues of a seated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jingdezhen is a prefecture-level city that kept the word zhen, town, in its name after it was upgraded in 1949, to honour its history. It was named one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s historical and cultural cities in 1982. In 2004 it marked 1,000 years as an imperial kiln centre and as Jingdezhen. It still makes tablewares in great quantity and costlier decorative pieces, and its Ceramic Institute teaches the craft. A blue and white jar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 2005 for $27.7 million.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jingdezhen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some worked for the government: the Ministry of Public Works hired artisans and labourers for temporary service, and the imperial kilns at <b>Jingdezhen</b> made the famous blue and white porcelain, while the Dehua factories in Fujian made export porcelain for European tastes by the late 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
